INT: And then what happened, after-- after the war started?

AK: Well, when-- when-- just before the war started, rumors were that something is cooking. And we moved to Warsaw. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] As we all know by now, Poland was taken over very quickly by the Germans. Half of it was occupied by the Germans, and the eastern half was occupied by the Russians.

The only place that was still fighting was Warsaw. Warsaw was very heavy defended. And we got stuck there. We spent-- I can remember that, on Rosh Hashanah, that September, we couldn't go-- because of this bombing, we couldn't go anymore to the synagogue, so we created services in the building. There were a lot of Jews living in the same building. And we had the services in the building, in one of the apartments. On Yom Kippur, we couldn't even use the apartment anymore, so the services took place in the cellar, in the-- in the air-raid shelter.

Eventually, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Warsaw was very heavy bombed. And eventually they have to give in. The house that we used to live was completely destroyed and burned down to ground. All the merchandise that my-- that my father sent to Warsaw to-- to be saved, everything was destroyed, including our personal effects. And we just got out with whatever we had on our-- on our backs.

We had to move to some other location. We had some other relatives, that we temporarily moved to them. We couldn't leave the city, because the-- the German troops didn't go in yet, and they didn't let anybody out. This took few days.

During these-- those days, we started already to feel the antisemitism of the Germans. Like, for instance, they used to distribute bread, and there were lines of people, standing to get this bread, and the Germans were passing by and asking if there are any Jews among those people, and they had to leave the lines. They didn't give any bread to-- to the Jews.

Immediately after the German troops marched in to the city, we moved out-- on foot. We started to walk out. The trains were not running yet. Nothing was running.

We managed to get to a-- a little town outside Warsaw called Pruszków. And we were told that, from that town, the trains are already moving in some capacity. The following day, we went to the railroad station, and there were some trains. Most of them were freight trains, not-- not even passenger trains. But, at that point, we couldn't care less-- as long as we had something to move. And we were trying to get back to our hometown. To be more specifically, by 'we,' I mean, it was my mother, my sister, my uncle and his wife, and myself. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

Eventually took us about three days to come back to my hometown. This is a trip that, normally, you-- should have taken about four or five hours. It took us three days.

It was really something fascinating. When I walked out, in the middle of the night, from the train station, in-- in town, where-- where I used to live for 10 years-- I was about 10 years old at that time-- I-- I couldn't recognize the town! The whole town was covered with Nazi flags and slogans.

I have to mention that this town had quite a large German ethnic population. This town was actually German during World War I. And therefore, the Germans, when they occupied this part of Poland, they took this part of the country as a-- not as an-- they didn't consider this as an occupied territories. They were actually part of their old Germany. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

And we came back home. Of course, my father was very happy to see us back. He was trying to join us, just before the war started, but he didn't make it. He-- actually, he was already half a way-- traveling half a way to Warsaw, and then the train stops because of the fights, and he had to go back. He-- he didn't hear anything from us. There were no telephones-- no nothing. So, of course, he was very happy to have us back home.

A few days after that, the Germans decided to take all the male Jews-- I mean, the adult males-- and they told him they-- they have to registered, and they are going to be sent, for something like eight weeks, for certain labor. And they even got special cards to buy working uniforms-- overalls and shoes and so on.

And they-- they took them all. They put them on the train. And, what I have found out later on, they were all shipped east to the River Bug-- spelled B-U-G-- which was about the middle of Poland. And this was the river that divided the Russian-occupied territories and the German-occupied territories. And they told them they can go only east. If they will go west, south, or north, they will be shot.

And so, when they approached the bridges to cross the river, the Russians stopped them, of course. It was like a regular border. But eventually they managed somehow to sneak into the other side. And my father ended up in the city of Lwów, where he spent rest of the time on-- under the Soviet occupation-- until, of course, the Germans invaded the Soviet Union.

He managed somehow to communicate with us. There was, that time, no normal postal services, so he managed to send some letters to people who-- who were traveling back and forth. And he suggested that we should not stay in a large city during the war.

So we-- we moved to a neighboring city, called Sosnowiec, where my maternal grandmother used to live. My maternal grandfather, at that time, died shortly after the outbreak of the war. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And, again, from there, we moved to another city, called Wolbrom, which was a small town, and we stayed there for a while.

Approximately, I would say, a year-- a year after we arrived to that city, my mother decided to send us, for a little while, back to our grandmother. And she stayed in that little city, Wolbrom. Myself and my sister were taken by a Christian woman, and-- because the Jews were not allowed to travel. And we went back to Sosnowiec, where I-- where I spent another almost two years, living with my grandmother. Means, myself and my sister.

It was really very tough, because we were there, so to speak, illegal. We were not registered. And therefore, we did not have our ration cards, to-- to get the-- whatever there were-- whatever food they were distributing, officially. So additional food had to be purchased on the black market.

I managed to get some schooling-- which, again, was forbidden for the Jewish population but was done privately at homes. Of course, this was illegal, so we had to be very careful not to be caught. And we had books left over from-- from the students from before the war, and this is what we were studying-- you know, the regular subject which normally were taught in-- in schools. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

I was in a constant letter communication with my mother, on one side, and my father, who was in Lwów, on the other side. Then, when the war continue with Soviet Union, the Germans invaded the-- the occupied part of Poland by-- by the Soviets. And my father, whom I have met later on, he-- he had a choice-- actually, they forced him to receive the Soviet citizenship. Because if he wouldn't, then they would have sent him to what at that time they called-- that they are being sent to Siberia. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Looking on it now, it would be better if he would have been sent to Siberia. Because most of the people who were sent there, actually they either did go to Siberia or ended up in-- in China, Japan, Tehran and-- and somehow survived the war. He wanted to come back to us, so he decided to get the Soviet citizenship, and they left him in town. They didn't deported him.

But then he got stuck with the invading Germans. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] After the Germans invaded Lwów, he asked permission to join us. Somehow, they didn't let him to go directly to us, so he went to-- to Cracow, where he had his family.

And this was about the time-- or actually, it was exactly the time-- of my bar mitzvah. I was prepared to my bar mitzvah by a teacher who came from the same town that I came from. And he knew my father very well. He was a Hebrew teacher. And the bar mitzvah took place-- not in a synagogue, because all the synagogues were already burnt down. But the services were conducted in-- in private houses. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

On the bar mitzvah, the only one that attended was my-- from my family-- was my grandmother. I had an uncle. Excuse me. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] he was working for the-- for the Jewish community center. And he even had to work on Saturday, so he couldn't come to the-- to the bar-mitzvah ceremony.

But it was done the best-- the best it could have been done, under those circumstances. I even had a little party for my friends, after that, at home. And, at the same time, I received my congratulatory telegram from my father. This was actually the first message that I got from my father after he moved to-- to-- actually it was not Cracow, it was, again--

My father had a-- a theory, that it's not good to stay in a big town during the war. And he had a cousin who had a little house and a little general store in-- in a-- in a village near Cracow. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And he moved to that cousin's house, there. And, from there, he sent that telegram for my bar mitzvah. And eventually I got a letter from him.

And [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] shortly after my bar mitzvah, my-- my mother decided to take us back. This is one of the things I don't remember, how the thing came about, this-- this shuffling back and forth. But anyhow, again, a-- a Christian woman was sent to pick us up, my sister and myself. And we went over to that town, Wolbrom, where my mother stayed. And, after this, my father requested that we will be moved to his location, which he considered-- that he considered safer, being in a village rather than in a small town which had quite substantial Jewish population.

This was-- now we are talking, now, about summer of '42. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] This was the first time that I have seen my father since 1939, since the beginning of the war, when he was sent out from Katowice. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Of course, the-- the meeting was very emotional. And we stayed with him, in that village-- it was not quite comfortable, because the house was small, and he had a space on-- on an attic of the building. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

I forgot to mention that there was another uncle of mine, my father's brother, and his wife, who also ran away from Cracow. They didn't want to go to the ghetto, and they decided that it will be better to hide in a small village. And they were already there, in that-- in that house. So there were actually three families living in this one house, which also consist of that general store.

So we were living on an attic. But we were happy, because we were together. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] It was approximately two months that we were together with my father. And then rumors started to spread.

There were always rumors, before something came up, that something is going to happen. And [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] my father found out that the city where-- the-- the town where my mother is, still nothing happens there. So he decided to send us back-- back to her. I didn't want to leave him, until he promised me that he will hide himself, as well. But we couldn't go together. It was too big a crowd, you know, to travel. Jews were not allowed to travel.

[SIGHS] So, again, he took that Gentile woman who-- of course, she was paid for it, and she brought us back to our mother. He hide himself in a-- in another village. He had a friend who used to prepare-- he was in the business of providing Aryan papers and finding places-- hideout-- hiding places for Jews. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

He got in contact with him, and he-- he hide him in an other village, in a little-- in a-- in a-- in a barn-- which, according to what my father wrote me, later on, it was really awful. He couldn't stay there. He was waiting for the papers to be made, and waiting, waiting, and the papers didn't come. Eventually, he couldn't-- couldn't take it anymore, so he decided to go and to enter the Cracow ghetto, where he had a sister and, I think, some other relatives. My-- my father was originally from Cracow. He was born there. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

The conditions in the ghetto were [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] hard to describe. Anyway, he-- his sister did-- was living in somebody's else apartment already, and he had to join her. So-- so, was-- was really awful.

That time, when we went back to-- to my mother, she had a friend who was a doctor-- a physician. And she got very friendly with him. And doctors were usually always more protected than other people. And then she decided that she will stay with him-- including us-- and she will take over his name, so it will look like we are family.

And, from then on-- this is the second name that I mentioned, before-- my name was Gelberger, which was the doctor's name. And we were just as a family! He was, like, our father. [PAUSES FOR 9 SECONDS]

Sometimes after that, there was a big action in Wolbrom. By action, I mean deportation. They came in the morning. They were chasing everybody out-- I mean, only the Jews, of course-- were chasing out, from the-- from the houses and sending them to a-- to a big place near the railroad station. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

The doctor was excluded. He didn't have to go. There were living a certain group of people that did not supposed to go to that deportation. But because they didn't want to-- to leave the-- quote-unquote, the doctor's 'family,' the doctor decided to go with the family, together, to the deportation place.

