And, of course, they had to be paid very well. And, on foot, at night, we left the town. And somehow we managed to go back to the town where my grandmother was still living.

Again, it was very hard to-- to be there, because everybody had to had documents-- which we didn't have. Being so-called illegal, we didn't have our ration cards. But somehow we-- we survived. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

Now, just for the orientation, this is now the fall or close to the winter of 1942. The beginning of '43, we are now in Sosnowiec. They finally decided to create a ghetto.

The ghetto was a neighborhood outside-- outside the town that they evacuated all the Polish population from there and decided to-- to convert this to a Jewish ghetto. Actually, there were two-- two ghettos. Was-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] was, like, A ghetto and a B ghetto. And the population used to get cards which indicated to which ghetto they will be assigned to.

So we have to move the-- the original-- we-- we were still staying in my grandmother's apartment, which was her apartment from before the war. And we had to move to the ghetto.

INT: Was there a difference between the A ghetto and the B ghetto?

AK: Well, one was smaller, the other one was larger. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS] I don't remember, now, what the difference was, but it looked to us like the larger ghetto was safer than the smaller ghetto. Judging by the type of population that was assigned, it-- it was some kind of segregation, that you could feel that something is not the same in the-- in the B ghetto as in the A ghetto.

Incidentally, everybody had to be occupied, somehow. There were-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] there were factories, called shops, that people were engaged mainly in-- in the war industry, like making uniforms for the army and-- and other related industry-- maybe ammunition and things like this-- related industry to the-- to the war. Everybody was trying to-- to be at least registered or to be engaged in one of those project, hoping that this will save him from deportation-- that he will be essential, and-- and this will save him for-- from deportation.

Because the deportation were taking place constantly. every few months, there was another transport being sent, either to labor camps or to somewhere-- at that point, we still didn't know where. Later on, we found out, but, at that point, we didn't know. But, of course, we didn't like the deportation. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

