But they volunteered to stay, to continue to stay in camp. Now, people can ask, you know, how come-- who-- who would volunteer to stay? But they were smart.

Most of them were Germans-- young people. If they will be released now from the camp, they will go straight to the Russian frontier and probably not return from there. So, in the camp, there were already old-timers. They had a good job and good position and say, well, we'll rather stay here. They were allowed to grow their hair.

And he was one of them. When he was the head of the block, he-- he had his hair. But now, when I met him again in Mauthausen, he didn't have them anymore. He was shaved. He looked terrible.

The population was increasing, from day to day. They didn't have any more room in the camp. So what they did is, they built-- outside the camp, they had a-- a section which was surrounded with barbed wire. I don't think that the barbed wire was electrified, at that point, but it was barbed wire, heavily guarded with guards.

And they erected huge tents, like-- like-- like circus tents-- huge! And they put us all in those tents. Now, this is still earlier spring. It was very, very cold.

Again, they give us very little food. And most of the people over there were actually Hungarians who came directly from Hungary. They were-- they told me that they were engaged in building fortifications for the Germans, against the Soviet invasion. At some point, that even got to a point where they were given weapons, instructing them to shoot the Soviets when they are nearing. Then they decided they-- they will get rid of them, and they brought us-- they brought them to-- to our camp.

They were with their families-- women, and some children. Everything was mixed up. There was no labor involved. We didn't do any labor-- nothing.

At one point, which I remember very vividly-- and actually, this incident is also recorded in-- in Martin Gilbert's book, famous book about Holocaust-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] an American plane came by, and he saw a camp. He didn't-- he thought it might be a military camp, and he threw a bomb. All of a sudden, the following day, you could see-- in many places, you could see fires and people cooking meat. All of a sudden, there was meat, which, of course, you know, it's obvious cannibalism.

[SIGHS] Then we were really afraid to get the-- the-- the-- the food that they were serving us. You know, we-- we found some bone inside or some piece of meat, which usually used to be a horse meat. We were afraid it might be, you know, meat from-- from those victims.

