It was very easy to escape. The question is, where? I mean, the first house that you will walk in, they will send you right away, you know, to the police.

So we were just walking. And they brought us to a little-- little forest which had log cabins, built like a camp. It must have been some kind of-- of a-- of a abandoned military camp. And they put us there, in-- it-- it was raining and very cold. We couldn't be outside. We had to be inside the barracks, even if we didn't want.

And they squeezed us, in such a way that we-- we were sitting on the floor, and one person was sitting inside the other. If somebody had to get up and go out, if he came back he couldn't find his spot anymore. There were fights. [SIGHS]

Then we found out, later on, that they were giving-- they were giving us some kind of a soup which was very-- it was like a water, basically, but was very sweet. And then we found out that the soup had poison in it. They were trying to poison us.

And we noticed that the number of deaths increased tremendously. You know, people were just falling down. We couldn't understand why. I mean, people were starving, but not-- not in such a frequency like at this point.

Until, one day, somebody came back, and he said that he does not see the guards anymore around us. Now, don't forget, this was a forest, and we couldn't see much, you know? You really had to go out a little bit, to see if there is anybody out. And he said he doesn't see any more any guards.

And then he came back. He said, that's it. Everybody ran away. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] This was the liberation. It was May 4, Friday, late afternoon.

And it came to me not really like a shock, something that I cannot explain, but it-- it just came to me very natural. It's-- it's-- like, it-- it had to come! This was it.

This was the first night that it really didn't rain, and I managed to go to sleep outside the barrack-- didn't had to squeeze inside that barrack. And, the following day, I walked out.

And I have seen all those people, laying and dying. And this is really what hit me, you know, that they are dying now. If they would have died before, well, this was the-- what we were going through. But they managed to-- to live until the moment of liberation, and now they have to die. This affect me even more than-- than anything else.

I walked out to the main road. And, all of a sudden, I realized that I am alone. Until now, I was always with my parents or with one of my parents, and they were telling me, you know, where to go, what to do. Now, I have to decide by myself where to go.

And well, the decision was made for me quickly, by one of the American military policemen, who direct the whole traffic in one direction because, the other direction, they were still fighting. So, we had to go. And we were walking on the side of the road, while the American tracks-- American troop, in trucks-- were passing by and throwing us cans of food and cigarettes and chocolate.

And this was another part, that-- that-- that people who were starving, all those-- all those days, all of a sudden got this big, rich American food and started to eat. And many of them got sick. We-- we-- eventually, we managed to get to a-- a-- to a huge storage house which stored food for the entire area. And, of course, you know, we broke the door and we just help ourself.

There were thousands of thousands of people, all from that camp. And I could have seen, you know-- I-- I-- one incident I remember in particular-- a-- a man who got a big can of meat. He opened up the can. He had a knife. He opened up the can, and he finished the whole can. It was something like maybe a two-pound can.

He consumed this, in one shot. And he dropped dead, with the can still holding in the hand. I mean, his stomach couldn't take it-- something like this. I--

